<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 7 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">The World Wars</h1><p class="path-tagline">Three decades that ended the age of empires.</p><p class="path-intro">Between 1914 and 1945 the states of Europe fought two wars that consumed the world and, in the end, their own empires. This path follows the arc from Sarajevo to Hiroshima: the war that broke four empires, the peace that failed, and the war that finished what the first began.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Strategist</a> · <a href="/start">the Timekeeper</a></p><p class="path-print-row"><button type="button" class="ws-print path-print">Print this path</button></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-world-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Encyclopedia · Event</span><span class="path-step-name">First World War</span><span class="path-step-why">Four years that killed four empires: Hohenzollern, Habsburg, Romanov and Ottoman.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The First World War (1914–1918) was a global conflict between the Allied Powers, including Britain, France and Russia, and the Central Powers, including Germany, Austria-Hungary and the Ottoman Empire.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Encyclopedia · Event</span><span class="path-step-name">Treaty of Versailles</span><span class="path-step-why">The peace that ended the war and seeded the next.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Treaty of Versailles was the peace treaty signed in 1919 between the Allied powers and Germany after the First World War.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/german-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/proclamation-of-the-german-empire.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Profile · 1871 CE – 1918 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">German Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">The rising power whose fears and ambitions drove the July crisis of 1914.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The German Empire was the unified German state proclaimed in 1871 under Prussian leadership after the Franco-Prussian War.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/empire-of-japan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-meiji.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Profile · 1868 CE – 1945 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Empire of Japan</span><span class="path-step-why">The one non-European empire of the age, from Meiji ascent to 1945.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Empire of Japan was the Japanese state from the Meiji Restoration of 1868 until the post-war constitution of 1947.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-world-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Encyclopedia · Event</span><span class="path-step-name">Second World War</span><span class="path-step-why">The deadliest war in history, and the end of Europe's centuries at the centre of the world.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Second World War was the global conflict of 1939 to 1945 between the Allies and the Axis powers, the deadliest war in history.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/atomic-bombings-of-hiroshima-and-nagasaki"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/atomic-bombings-of-hiroshima-and-nagasaki.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1945</span><span class="path-step-name">Atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki</span><span class="path-step-why">The weeks that ended the war and opened an age in which great-power war risks everything.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki were the attacks by the United States on two Japanese cities in August 1945, the only uses of nuclear weapons in war.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/british-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/slavery-abolition-act-1833.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Profile · 1583 CE – 1997 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">British Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Victorious and exhausted: how winning two world wars still cost an empire.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The British Empire comprised the dominions, colonies, protectorates and other territories ruled or administered by Britain from the late sixteenth to the twentieth century.</span></span></a></li></ol>
